# Batch 39 (2026-11-26): Mesoamerica; Aboriginal Australia and Polynesia; China and Central Asia; Near East, Norse, cryptids, hoaxes and Cold War secrets
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None,statusNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  if statusNote:d['statusNote']=statusNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: Mesoamerica
E+=[
X('coyolxauhqui-stone-1978','The Coyolxauhqui Stone: electricians find a dismembered goddess and the Great Temple (1978)',H,'confirmed',2,1978,['archaeological','textual'],
 "On 21 February 1978 workers from Mexico City's electricity company, digging beside the Metropolitan Cathedral, struck a huge round stone covered in reliefs. It showed Coyolxauhqui, a goddess of Mexica (Aztec) myth, naked, beheaded and with her limbs cut off. In the myth, Coyolxauhqui led her 400 brothers to kill their mother Coatlicue on the hill of Coatepec, but the war god Huitzilopochtli was born fully armed, killed her and threw her body down the hill, where it broke apart. The find led the archaeologist Eduardo Matos Moctezuma to direct large-scale excavations that uncovered the Templo Mayor (Huey Teocalli), the twin temple of Huitzilopochtli and the rain god Tlaloc that the Spanish had demolished in 1521. The stone lay at the foot of Huitzilopochtli's stairway, where the bodies of sacrificed captives are recorded as having been thrown down.",
 [('Myth turned into architecture','Matos Moctezuma argued that Huitzilopochtli\'s half of the temple was built as a model of the mountain of Coatepec, so that each sacrifice re-enacted the god\'s victory over his sister; most scholars accept the link between the stone\'s position and the myth, though how literally rituals re-enacted it is debated.'),
  ('Was she the Moon?','Popular accounts often say Huitzilopochtli threw her head into the sky to become the Moon, making the myth a battle of sun against moon and stars. Some scholars note that the lunar identification is not clearly stated in the earliest Nahuatl sources and may be a later or modern interpretation.'),
  ('A city still buried','The find was an accident during routine utility work. Later chance discoveries nearby, such as the giant Tlaltecuhtli monolith (2006) and the Huey Tzompantli skull rack (2015), show how much of Tenochtitlan still lies beneath modern Mexico City.')],
 ['wiki:Coyolxāuhqui','wiki:Templo_Mayor','wiki:Eduardo_Matos_Moctezuma'],img='File:Mexico-3980 - Coyolxauhqui Stone (2508259597).jpg',dateNote='Stone carved c. 1470s; found 21 February 1978'),
X('aztec-sun-stone','The Aztec Sun Stone: calendar, altar or cosmic map?',H,'confirmed',2,1502,['archaeological','iconographic'],
 "The Sun Stone (Piedra del Sol), often called the 'Aztec Calendar', is a basalt disc about 3.6 m across and weighing about 24 tonnes, carved by the Mexica, probably between 1502 and 1521. After the Spanish conquest it was buried in Mexico City's main square; according to the chronicler Diego Durán, Archbishop Alonso de Montúfar ordered this so that the memory of the sacrifices performed there would be lost. It was rediscovered on 17 December 1790 during repaving work. The scholar Antonio de León y Gama persuaded the cathedral not to rebury it and published one of the first studies of Mexican archaeology about it in 1792; it was set into the cathedral's west tower until 1885 and is now the centrepiece of the National Museum of Anthropology. Around a central face are the glyphs for the four previous world ages (the 'Suns') and the 20 day-signs of the calendar.",
 [('Who is in the centre?','The face is usually identified as the sun god Tonatiuh, holding human hearts in clawed hands with a sacrificial knife for a tongue. Art historian Richard Townsend and others argued it is the earth deity Tlaltecuhtli, and some suggest a night-sun god; the identification remains debated.'),
  ('Not a working calendar','Although it shows calendar signs, scholars agree it was not used to tell dates. Many now think it served as a ritual altar or a platform for sacrifice (it has a similar form to temalacatl gladiatorial stones), using calendar imagery to show the cycles of cosmic time that sacrifice was believed to sustain.'),
  ('Prophecy and lost knowledge','Popular and alternative writers have read the stone as a record of past world catastrophes or as encoding precise astronomical knowledge and predictions of the end of the present age. Archaeologists accept that it depicts the Mexica myth of world ages, but see no evidence that it records actual past catastrophes or hidden astronomical data.')],
 ['wiki:Aztec_sun_stone','wiki:Antonio_de_León_y_Gama'],img='Aztec sun stone',end=1521,dateNote='Carved c. 1502-1521; buried after 1521; rediscovered 17 December 1790'),
X('moctezuma-headdress','Moctezuma\'s headdress: the feathered treasure in Vienna',H,'disputed',2,1515,['physical object','museum records'],
 "A spectacular featherwork piece of green quetzal, turquoise cotinga and other feathers with small gold ornaments is held by the Weltmuseum in Vienna. It has been in Austrian collections since the late 16th century, when it belonged to Archduke Ferdinand II of Tyrol at Ambras Castle and was rediscovered there in the late 19th century by Ferdinand von Hochstetter for Vienna's new museum. It is one of only a handful of surviving pre-Hispanic featherworks. A tradition, popular since the 19th century, holds that it belonged to the emperor Moctezuma II and was among gifts sent by Hernán Cortés to Charles V. A joint Austrian-Mexican team studied and restored it from 2010 to 2012. Mexico has repeatedly asked for its return or loan, most recently in 2020; Austria has declined, citing a conservation study that concluded vibration during transport could damage it.",
 [('Moctezuma\'s own?','No document directly links the object to Moctezuma, and early inventories do not call it a headdress; some researchers have suggested it may have been a cape, a priest\'s regalia or a military standard. Supporters of the Moctezuma tradition point to its exceptional quality and the recorded gifts from Cortés to Europe.'),
  ('Too fragile to travel, or too valuable to return?','Austrian experts say the feathers and fragile structure make transport too risky; critics in Mexico see the argument as convenient and propose solutions such as special vibration-proof cases. A replica made in 1940 stands in Mexico City\'s National Museum of Anthropology.'),
  ('A symbol','In the 20th century the piece became an emblem of Mexico\'s indigenous heritage and of wider debates about returning objects taken in colonial times.')],
 ['wiki:Moctezuma\'s_headdress','wiki:Weltmuseum_Wien'],img='Moctezuma\'s headdress',end=2012,dateNote='Made c. 1515; recorded at Ambras 1596; restored 2010-2012'),
X('la-malinche','La Malinche (Malintzin): interpreter, traitor or survivor?',H,'disputed',2,1519,['textual','codices'],
 "Malintzin, baptised Marina and known as La Malinche (c. 1500 - c. 1529), was a Nahua woman who had been sold or given into slavery in the Maya lands of Tabasco. In 1519 she was one of twenty enslaved women given to Hernán Cortés's expedition. Because she spoke both Nahuatl and Yucatec Maya, she and the shipwrecked Spaniard Gerónimo de Aguilar formed a translation chain (Spanish-Maya-Nahuatl), until she learned Spanish herself. She became Cortés's chief interpreter, adviser and intermediary during the conquest of the Aztec Empire (1519-21); Indigenous pictorial records such as the Lienzo de Tlaxcala show her standing at Cortés's side, and the Nahua reportedly called Cortés himself 'Malinche'. She bore Cortés a son, Martín, one of the first mestizos of New Spain, and later married a Spanish hidalgo.",
 [('Traitor','After Mexican independence (1821) writers increasingly cast her as a traitor to her people; the word malinchista still means someone who prefers the foreign to the native, and Octavio Paz (1950) made her a symbol of a violated mother of the nation.'),
  ('Survivor and victim','Many historians and Chicana feminists stress that she was an enslaved teenager with no people of her own to betray, and that the Nahua-speaking peoples were themselves divided, with Tlaxcala and other cities allied with Cortés against the Mexica.'),
  ('Agent','Others emphasise her skill and influence, arguing that she shaped events such as the alliance with Tlaxcala and the massacre at Cholula; Bernal Díaz claimed she uncovered a Cholulan plot, a story some historians regard as a Spanish justification for the killings.')],
 ['wiki:La_Malinche','wiki:Lienzo_de_Tlaxcala'],img='La Malinche',end=1529,dateNote='c. 1500 - c. 1529; with Cortés 1519-1521'),
X('noche-triste-1520','La Noche Triste (1520) and the lost gold of Tenochtitlan',H,'confirmed',2,1520,['textual','archaeological','X-ray fluorescence'],
 "In 1520, after Pedro de Alvarado massacred Mexica nobles during a festival at the Templo Mayor and the emperor Moctezuma II died in Spanish captivity, the Spanish and their Tlaxcalan allies were besieged in Tenochtitlan. On the night of 30 June - 1 July 1520 Cortés tried to escape across the western causeway. They were discovered, and in the fighting on the causeways and canals hundreds of Spaniards and probably thousands of allies were killed; many soldiers drowned, weighed down by gold they had taken from the city's treasure. The Spanish called it La Noche Triste, 'the Night of Sorrows'; in Mexico it is now officially also called the 'Victorious Night'. On 13 March 1981 a construction worker found a gold bar of 1.93 kg at a depth of 4.8 m near the Alameda Central, along the line of the escape route. X-ray fluorescence analysis published by INAH in 2020 showed its alloy matches late Mexica gold, indicating it was cast in 1519-20 from melted Aztec goldwork.",
 [('How did Moctezuma die?','Spanish accounts say Moctezuma was killed by stones thrown by his own people as he urged them to stop fighting; several Indigenous accounts say the Spanish killed him. Historians have no way to settle the question.'),
  ('The rest of the treasure','Legends of Moctezuma\'s lost treasure, said to lie at the bottom of the lake or to have been hidden by the Mexica, have lasted for centuries. In 1521 the Spanish tortured the last emperor, Cuauhtémoc, by burning his feet to make him reveal gold that he said had been thrown into the lake; the gold bar of 1981 is the only find so far clearly tied to the retreat.'),
  ('Where Cortés wept','An old ahuehuete (cypress) tree in Popotla is traditionally said to be where Cortés sat and wept after the retreat; the tradition cannot be verified, and the tree has since been badly damaged by fire.')],
 ['wiki:La_Noche_Triste','url:https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/gold-bar-once-belonged-aztec-emperor-moctezuma-180973959/|Smithsonian Magazine: Spanish Conquistadors Stole This Gold Bar From Aztec Emperor Moctezuma\'s Trove (2020)|article','url:https://www.livescience.com/gold-bar-looted-aztec-treasure.html|Live Science: Massive Gold Bar Unearthed in Mexico Was Looted Aztec Treasure (2020)|article'],img='La Noche Triste',dateNote='30 June - 1 July 1520; gold bar found 1981, analysed 2019-20'),
X('dresden-codex','The Dresden Codex: a Maya book of Venus, eclipses and a world-ending flood?',H,'confirmed',2,1150,['textual','astronomical'],
 "The Dresden Codex is one of only four Maya books known to survive; most others were destroyed by Spanish missionaries, notably at the auto-da-fé organised by Bishop Diego de Landa at Maní in Yucatán in 1562. Painted on folded bark paper about 3.6 m long, it probably comes from the Chichén Itzá region and is usually dated to the 11th-14th century, copied from older texts. The Dresden royal librarian Johann Christian Götze bought it in Vienna in 1739. In the 1880s the librarian Ernst Förstemann worked out its number system and Long Count dates and showed that it contains highly accurate astronomical tables: a Venus table tracking the planet's 584-day cycle and a lunar table that predicts possible eclipse seasons. It was badly water-damaged when kept in a flooded basement during the bombing of Dresden in February 1945.",
 [('A flood at the end of the world?','The last page shows a sky serpent and an aged goddess pouring water. Förstemann interpreted it as the destruction of the world in a great flood, an idea later used in 2012 end-of-the-world claims. Many Mayanists now read it as a scene of rain and renewal tied to the calendar rather than a prophecy.'),
  ('Astronomers or astrologers?','The tables are precise enough that modern scholars still debate how the Maya corrected them over centuries. Their purpose was largely divinatory: Venus\'s appearances were thought to bring danger and to be good times for war.'),
  ('How much was lost','Landa wrote that the books contained "superstition and lies of the devil" and that their owners grieved greatly when they were burned; historians estimate that hundreds or thousands of books once existed. A fourth surviving book, the Maya Codex of Mexico (formerly the Grolier Codex), was long suspected of being a fake until it was authenticated in 2016.')],
 ['wiki:Dresden_Codex','wiki:Maya_codices','wiki:2012_phenomenon'],img='Dresden Codex',end=1945,dateNote='Painted c. 11th-14th century; bought 1739; damaged 1945'),
X('calakmul-snake-kingdom','Calakmul and the Snake Kingdom: the Maya superpower that fought Tikal',H,'confirmed',2,636,['archaeological','epigraphic','lidar'],
 "Calakmul, deep in the forests of Campeche, Mexico, was reported in 1931 by the botanist Cyrus Lundell. Decipherment of Maya glyphs since the 1970s-90s revealed that it was the 7th-century seat of the Kaan ('Snake') dynasty, whose emblem glyph appears in more texts than any other. The Snake kings built a network of allies and vassals across the lowlands, including Caracol, Naranjo and Dos Pilas, to encircle their great rival Tikal. Under Yuknoom Ch'een II (ruled 636-686), Calakmul defeated Tikal in 657 and again in 677-679. But in August 695 Tikal's king Jasaw Chan K'awiil defeated Calakmul's Yuknoom Yich'aak K'ak' and captured the image of a Calakmul patron god, and Calakmul's power declined. The city had a great pyramid (Structure 2) over 45 m high, thousands of buildings and perhaps 50,000 people.",
 [('Superpowers or looser alliances?','Epigraphers Simon Martin and Nikolai Grube described Classic Maya politics as a struggle between two \'superstates\', Tikal and Calakmul. Others see a more fluid web of rival kingdoms, with \'overlordship\' sometimes ceremonial rather than real control.'),
  ('A wandering dynasty','Glyphs show Calakmul used a bat emblem before the 7th century, and the snake emblem is found earlier at Dzibanché in Quintana Roo; many epigraphers think the Kaan dynasty moved its capital to Calakmul around 630, while others argue for different sequences.'),
  ('Star wars','Some battles are recorded with a \'star war\' glyph. A popular theory says the Maya timed wars by the planet Venus; studies of the dates show only partial correlation, and the link remains debated.')],
 ['wiki:Calakmul','wiki:Yuknoom_Chʼeen_II'],img='Calakmul',end=695,dateNote='Kaan dynasty at Calakmul c. 630-8th century; defeat 695'),
X('yaxchilan-lady-xoc','Lady Xoc\'s vision serpent: blood, power and a Maya queen at Yaxchilan',H,'confirmed',2,723,['archaeological','epigraphic','iconographic'],
 "At Yaxchilan, on the Usumacinta River in Chiapas, Mexico, three carved stone door lintels in Structure 23 (c. 723-726) show Lady K'abal Xook (Lady Xoc, died 742), the principal wife of King Itzamnaaj Bahlam III (Shield Jaguar, ruled 681-742). On Lintel 24 the king holds a torch while she pulls a rope studded with obsidian blades through her tongue, dripping blood onto paper in a basket. On Lintel 25 smoke from the burned paper rises as a giant 'vision serpent', from whose jaws emerges an armed warrior, often read as an ancestor or the dynasty's founder. Women seldom appear as the central figures of Maya monuments, which makes the lintels a key source on royal women's role in ritual and politics. The British explorer Alfred Maudslay had Lintels 24 and 25 cut out and shipped to Britain in 1882; they are in the British Museum.",
 [('Visions from blood loss?','Some researchers suggest that severe blood loss, fasting and possibly other substances produced real hallucinations, so that the serpent records an altered-state experience; others read the serpent as a symbolic portal for summoning ancestors, without implying a literal vision.'),
  ('Politics in stone','The king\'s eventual heir, Bird Jaguar IV, was the son of another wife and only took the throne years after Shield Jaguar\'s death. Scholars debate whether Structure 23 was built to honour Lady Xoc\'s powerful lineage, and whether her prominence reflects a rivalry over the succession.'),
  ('Whose heritage?','Mexico has called for the return of objects removed in the 19th century; the lintels remain in London, part of wider repatriation debates.')],
 ['wiki:Lady_Xoc','wiki:Yaxchilan_Lintel_24','wiki:Yaxchilan'],img='File:Yaxchilan Lintel 24.jpg',end=742,dateNote='Lintels carved c. 723-726; Lady Xoc died 742'),
X('tlaloc-rain-god','Tlaloc, the rain god who brought a storm to Mexico City (1964)',M,'myth-legend',2,1400,['archaeological','textual','news reports'],
 "Tlaloc was the Aztec god of rain, lightning and fertility, and one of the oldest gods of central Mexico: goggle-eyed, fanged rain gods appear at Teotihuacan centuries earlier. He shared the summit of Tenochtitlan's Templo Mayor with Huitzilopochtli, and his most important shrine stood on Mount Tlaloc (about 4,100 m) on the eastern rim of the Valley of Mexico. Those who died by drowning, lightning or water-related illness were believed to go to Tlalocan, a green paradise of endless spring. Sacrifices, including of children whose tears were thought to bring rain, were offered to him and his helpers, the tlaloque. A colossal unfinished stone figure of about 165 tonnes lay in a stream bed near the village of Coatlinchan. When the Mexican government moved it to the new National Museum of Anthropology on 16 April 1964, despite local protests in which villagers sabotaged the transport, it arrived in Mexico City at night in an unusually heavy storm in the dry season.",
 [('The god\'s revenge?','Newspapers at the time and many visitors since have half-jokingly attributed the 1964 downpour to the god; residents of Coatlinchan have said the rains have not been the same since the statue left. Meteorologically the storm was a coincidence, though an unusual one for April.'),
  ('Tlaloc or Chalchiuhtlicue?','Some scholars and the 19th-century painter José María Velasco identified the colossus as Tlaloc\'s female partner, the water goddess Chalchiuhtlicue, and its identity is still discussed.'),
  ('Child sacrifice','Spanish friars described children being sacrificed to Tlaloc. Excavations of an offering at the Templo Mayor containing the remains of dozens of children support those accounts, while scholars debate how frequent and widespread such rites were.')],
 ['wiki:Tláloc','wiki:Coatlinchan','url:https://mexicolore.co.uk/aztecs/stories/tlalocs-great-journey|Mexicolore: Tlaloc\'s Great Journey|article','url:https://www.jornada.com.mx/2014/04/21/cultura/a07n1cul|La Jornada (2014): A Coatlinchán sólo le quedó el orgullo de ser sitio original de Tláloc|news'],img='File:Monolito de Tláloc, Museo Nacional de Antropología.jpg',end=1964,dateNote='Worshipped c. 1st millennium CE-1521; monolith moved 16 April 1964'),
X('xochicalco','Xochicalco: the hilltop city of the feathered serpent and the zenith-sun cave',H,'confirmed',3,700,['archaeological','astronomical'],
 "Xochicalco ('in the place of the house of flowers') in Morelos, Mexico, was a fortified hilltop city that flourished in the Epiclassic period (about 650-900 CE), after the fall of Teotihuacan, and may have had up to 20,000 inhabitants. Its art blends styles from Teotihuacan, the Maya region and central Mexico. Its most famous building, the Temple of the Feathered Serpent, is carved with undulating plumed serpents, seated lords in Maya-like poses and glyphs of towns, possibly tributaries. A cave modified with a vertical shaft served as an observatory: on about 14-15 May and 28-29 July, when the sun passes through the zenith at noon, its light shines straight down onto the cave floor. Around 900 CE the city was burned and abandoned suddenly. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
 [('A congress of astronomers?','Some researchers interpret the temple\'s reliefs and calendar glyphs as commemorating a meeting of priests from different regions to correct or harmonise their calendars; others see them as records of tribute, conquest or dynastic ritual.'),
  ('Birthplace of the Quetzalcoatl cult?','Because the feathered serpent dominates its art, some historians see Xochicalco as an important link between the Teotihuacan feathered-serpent imagery and the later Toltec and Aztec Quetzalcoatl; how direct the connection was is uncertain.'),
  ('Who destroyed it?','The burning of the city may reflect internal revolt or attack by rivals; there is no consensus.')],
 ['wiki:Xochicalco','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/939/|UNESCO: Archaeological Monuments Zone of Xochicalco|heritage listing'],img='Xochicalco',end=900,dateNote='c. 650-900 CE'),
]
# --- part 2: Aboriginal Australia and Polynesia
E+=[
X('baiame-sky-father','Baiame, the sky father of south-eastern Australia',M,'myth-legend',2,1830,['oral tradition','rock art','ethnographic records'],
 "In the Dreaming of several Aboriginal peoples of south-eastern Australia, including the Wiradjuri, Kamilaroi (Gamilaraay), Eora, Darkinjung and Wonnarua, Baiame (Biame, Baayami) is a creator and sky father. He came down from the sky, shaped rivers, mountains and forests, gave people laws and ceremonies, and then returned to the sky. He is closely linked to bora grounds, where boys were initiated, and is often shown in rock art as a large human figure with a big head-dress and outstretched arms; a painted rock shelter near Milbrodale in the Hunter Valley, known as Baiame Cave, is widely thought to depict him. In some traditions one of his wives is associated with the emu, and his son Daramulan also has a role in initiation.",
 [('Christian influence?','Missionaries such as William Ridley adopted the name Baiame for the Christian God when translating into Gamilaraay, and some anthropologists have argued that the idea of a single \'All-Father\' was reshaped, or even created, in response to contact with Christianity. Others, citing the writer K. Langloh Parker, note that belief in Baiame is attested by around 1830, before missionary activity in the region, and that Aboriginal knowledge-holders describe him as long pre-dating colonisation.'),
  ('Sky hero and astronomy','Some researchers link Baiame and his family to stars and constellations, as part of a wider body of Aboriginal sky knowledge; the details vary between nations and sources.'),
  ('Restricted knowledge','Much Baiame lore belonged to initiated men and was not to be seen by women or outsiders, so published versions are fragmentary and filtered through colonial recorders.')],
 ['wiki:Baiame','wiki:Bora_(Australian)'],img='File:BaiameCaveBulga0004.jpg',dateNote='Ancient oral tradition; attested in writing from c. 1830'),
X('tiddalik-frog','Tiddalik, the frog who drank all the water',M,'myth-legend',3,1870,['oral tradition','ethnographic records'],
 "Tiddalik is a story recorded in the late 19th century from the Gunaikurnai people near Port Albert in South Gippsland, Victoria, and since retold across Australia. A giant frog wakes up thirsty and drinks every lake, river and swamp until the land is parched and other animals are dying. The animals try to make him laugh so he will release the water; after others fail, an eel (Nabunum in some versions) twists itself into comical shapes and Tiddalik bursts out laughing, letting the water gush back. In some early versions the water comes out as a great flood that drowns many creatures and strands others on islands. The story is often linked to Australia's water-holding frogs, which store water in their bodies to survive drought and were sometimes dug up by Aboriginal people as a source of water.",
 [('A memory of a different landscape?','The water-holding frog associated with Tiddalik today is not found in Gippsland. Some writers have suggested the story reflects a time 10,000-12,000 years ago when the environment was different and the frog\'s range was larger; others think it refers to another frog, or that the link to the water-holding frog is a modern addition. Both remain speculative.'),
  ('A flood myth','Folklorists note that the 19th-century versions emphasise drought followed by flood, placing Tiddalik among the world\'s many stories of water withheld and released, rather than the gentle children\'s tale of modern picture books.'),
  ('Remade over time','Researchers have shown how the story changed as it was retold by settler writers and in children\'s books, with different animals making Tiddalik laugh; Aboriginal communities continue to tell their own versions.')],
 ['wiki:Tiddalik','wiki:Water-holding_frog'],img='File:Fish out of water … as Tiddalik watches. Condamine River, Warwick, 2022.jpg',dateNote='Recorded late 19th century; traditional story of unknown age'),
X('kow-swamp-burials','Kow Swamp: robust Ice Age skulls and the "two peoples" debate',H,'disputed',2,-20000,['archaeological','physical anthropology','luminescence dating'],
 "At Kow Swamp (Ghow Swamp) in northern Victoria, the anthropologist Alan Thorne excavated the partial remains of more than 22 people between 1968 and 1972, the largest known group of late Pleistocene burials in Australia. Some skulls had thick bones, heavy brow ridges and flat, receding foreheads that looked very different from the more lightly built ('gracile') people found at Lake Mungo and Keilor. Thorne argued that Australia had been settled by two different populations, one descended from Homo erectus of Java and one from China, which later mixed to form Aboriginal Australians. First dated to about 13,000-9,000 years ago, the cemetery was redated in 2003 by optically stimulated luminescence to about 22,000-19,000 years ago. After a campaign by Aboriginal communities, Museum Victoria returned the remains to the Yorta Yorta people in 1990 and they were reburied. The site was declared a protected Aboriginal place in 2022.",
 [('Two migrations','Thorne\'s model, part of the wider \'multiregional\' theory of human origins, held that the robust features showed continuity with Javan Homo erectus. Genetic studies of Aboriginal Australians since the 2010s point to a single founding population, and most researchers no longer accept a separate erectus-derived population.'),
  ('Head shaping','Peter Brown and others argued that some of the flattened foreheads were caused by deliberately binding infants\' heads, a practice known from other cultures, rather than by ancestry; Thorne and colleagues disputed this.'),
  ('Ice Age life','Other researchers explain the variation as the result of local adaptation and harsh Last Glacial Maximum conditions. Because the remains were reburied and many were fragmentary, the debate now depends largely on earlier measurements and casts.')],
 ['wiki:Ghow_Swamp_Aboriginal_Place','doi:10.1016/s0047-2484(03)00087-3','wiki:Multiregional_origin_of_modern_humans'],end=-9000,dateNote='Burials c. 22,000-19,000 years ago (OSL, 2003); earlier estimate 13,000-9,000 years ago; excavated 1968-72; reburied 1990'),
X('wilgie-mia-ochre-mine','Wilgie Mia: the red ochre mine worked for tens of thousands of years',H,'confirmed',3,-30000,['archaeological','oral tradition','heritage records'],
 "Wilgie Mia, known to the Wajarri Yamatji people as Thuwarri Thaa ('red ochre hole'), is an ochre mine in the Weld Range of Western Australia. Aboriginal miners used wooden wedges, hammerstones and scaffolding to dig a vast open cut and caves into the hill, removing an estimated 14,000-19,600 cubic metres of ochre and rock; the government heritage listing notes 'stop and pillar' supports and pole scaffolding with platforms, techniques not recorded at other traditional ochre mines. Estimates of how long it has been worked range from about 27,000 to 40,000 years, making it a candidate for the world's oldest continuously used mine. Its ochre was prized for ceremony and was traded across much of Western Australia and reportedly as far as 1,600 km away, the most extensive pre-contact ochre trade network recorded in Australia. In Wajarri tradition the mine was created by Marlu, the red kangaroo: the red ochre is his blood, the yellow his liver and the green his gall. It is a men's site and access is restricted. From the 1940s to the 1970s European miners also extracted ochre from the site. It was added to the Australian National Heritage List in 2011, and in 2018 the Wajarri Yamatji won exclusive native title over it.",
 [('How old?','Precise dating of the earliest mining is difficult because the mine has been reworked repeatedly; the wide range of age estimates reflects this uncertainty.'),
  ('Sacred and dangerous','Traditional rules governed who could enter and how ochre was taken; the Mondong, malevolent spirits, are said to guard the ochre, and the rituals that protected miners are better recorded here than at any other Aboriginal ochre mine.'),
  ('Ochre and the first art','Ochre use is one of the oldest signs of symbolic behaviour among modern humans, from Blombos Cave in South Africa to Australian rock art; Wilgie Mia shows the scale that ochre production could reach.')],
 ['wiki:Wilgie_Mia','url:https://www.dcceew.gov.au/parks-heritage/heritage/places/national/wilgie-mia|Australian Government (DCCEEW): Wilgie Mia Aboriginal Ochre Mine, National Heritage List|heritage listing'],end=None,dateNote='Worked for c. 27,000-40,000 years; heritage-listed 2011'),
X('gympie-pyramid','The Gympie "Pyramid": Egyptians, Chinese fleets or an old vineyard?',H,'debunked',3,1975,['archaeological','historical records','claims in popular books'],
 "Rocky Ridge, a terraced sandstone hill about 5 km north-east of Gympie in Queensland, known to the Kabi Kabi (Gubbi Gubbi) people as Djaki Kundu, became famous as the 'Gympie Pyramid'. In 1975 the writer Rex Gilroy claimed it was the remains of a pyramid built by ancient Egyptians who had mined gold in Australia, citing an unusual stone wall, local taboos, exotic cactus and a strange carved stone figure (the 'Gympie Ape') reportedly found nearby. Others attributed it to Phoenicians, the Maya or extraterrestrials, and Gavin Menzies later claimed it as a Ming Chinese observation platform from the voyages he believed reached Australia in 1421. Historical research shows that the low stone terraces were built by 19th-century settlers, most likely as a vineyard: a local settler wrote about vine-growing in the Gympie Times in 1884, and the paper described the area as 'the old vineyard' in 1905.",
 [('Lost visitors','Supporters of ancient-contact theories see the terraces, the figure and inscriptions as traces of pre-European visitors to Australia; no excavation has produced material supporting this, and the carved figure\'s origin is unknown.'),
  ('Aboriginal significance','When a highway upgrade crossed the area from 2020, some Kabi Kabi people and supporters argued that the hill was a significant Aboriginal site, citing grinding grooves; others disputed the claim, and the site\'s cultural status became a separate debate from the pyramid theories.'),
  ('A pattern','Researchers place it alongside other claimed Australian pyramids and \'lost race\' theories that attribute old structures in colonised lands to outsiders.')],
 ['wiki:Gympie_Pyramid','wiki:Gavin_Menzies'],img='Gympie Pyramid',dateNote='Terraces 19th century; pyramid claim 1975'),
X('marree-man-1998','The Marree Man: a 2.7 km giant drawn on the desert by unknown hands (1998)',H,'confirmed',2,1998,['aerial photography','anonymous faxes','physical evidence'],
 "On 26 June 1998 the charter pilot Trec Smith, flying between Marree and Coober Pedy in South Australia, spotted a gigantic outline of an Aboriginal man hunting with a throwing stick, cut into the surface of a plateau at Finniss Springs. The figure is about 2.7 km tall with a 28 km outline, making it one of the largest geoglyphs in the world. Nobody has ever claimed responsibility. Anonymous faxes sent to media and a local hotel called it 'Stuart's Giant', after the explorer John McDouall Stuart, and contained references to American places and spellings; in January 1999 another fax led officials to a buried plaque bearing a small US flag, the Olympic rings and the words 'In honour of the land they once knew'. Native title holders, the Arabana people, took legal action and the site was closed to visitors. The lines faded over time, and in 2016 local businesses, with the Arabana Aboriginal Corporation's consent, re-graded it using GPS.",
 [('American military?','The site lies just outside the Woomera Prohibited Area, and early speculation pointed to US personnel at nearby bases; investigators noted that the American clues might be deliberate red herrings.'),
  ('The artist theory','The Alice Springs artist Bardius Goldberg (died 2002) was suspected by friends of being involved, reportedly saying he had been paid to keep quiet; this has never been proven, and others suggest a small team with surveying equipment.'),
  ('Art, vandalism or insult?','Some Arabana people objected to an image of an Aboriginal man being carved into their country without permission, while others came to see it as a tourism asset. Like crop circles, it shows how anonymous land art can generate long-lasting mystery.')],
 ['wiki:Marree_Man'],img='Marree Man',dateNote='Discovered 26 June 1998; re-graded 2016'),
X('knowles-nullarbor-1988','The Knowles family and the "UFO" on the Nullarbor (1988)',H,'disputed',3,1988,['eyewitness','police statements','vehicle examination'],
 "In the early hours of 20 January 1988 Faye Knowles and her three adult sons were driving east across the Nullarbor Plain on the Eyre Highway, about 40 km west of Mundrabilla in Western Australia. They reported that a glowing object 'like an egg in an egg cup' chased their car, landed on the roof, lifted the car off the road and shook it, while their voices sounded distorted; when it dropped the car a tyre burst, and they fled into the bush. They drove on to Ceduna in South Australia and reported it to police, who said the car was dented and covered in an ash-like substance and that they were taking the report seriously. A truck driver also reported a strange light, and the story made headlines worldwide.",
 [('A genuine encounter','The family maintained their account, and some UFO researchers regard it as one of Australia\'s strongest physical-trace cases because of the multiple witnesses, the damage and the reported dust.'),
  ('Mirage and a blowout','Investigators, including UFO researcher Keith Basterfield and astronomer A. T. Brunt (writing in The Skeptic, 1989), concluded that the light was probably a mirage of distant truck headlights caused by a temperature inversion, and that a high-speed tyre blowout explains the jolting and brake dust; forensic tests of the car found nothing unusual.'),
  ('How stories grow','Sceptics note how press reports quickly added details such as the car being turned around; believers reply that the debunking explanations do not account for everything the witnesses described.')],
 ['url:https://www.abc.net.au/news/2018-02-23/curious-adelaide-ufo-sightings-across-australia/9466950|ABC News (2018): South Australia\'s X-Files - Curious Adelaide cracks open our most mysterious UFO cases|news','url:https://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/article/101971256|Trove (National Library of Australia): "UFO encounter on Nullarbor Plain reported", 21 January 1988|newspaper','url:https://www.skeptic.org.uk/1989/12/from-the-archives-the-1988-nullarbor-ufo-mystery-solved/|The Skeptic (1989, A. T. Brunt): The 1988 Nullarbor UFO Mystery - Solved|article'],img='File:Nullarbor Plain Escarpment DSC04558.JPG',dateNote='20 January 1988'),
X('truganini-last-tasmanian-myth','Truganini and the myth of the "last Tasmanian"',H,'confirmed',2,1876,['textual','museum records','oral history'],
 "Truganini (c. 1812 - 8 May 1876) was a Nuenonne woman from Bruny Island in south-eastern Tasmania. As a teenager she lived through the Black War, in which colonial violence and disease devastated Tasmania's Aboriginal population. She became a guide for the official George Augustus Robinson on his expeditions to persuade the remaining Aboriginal people to move to the Wybalenna settlement on Flinders Island, where many died. Later, in the Port Phillip District, she joined a group of fugitives and was tried with four others for the murder of two whalers, and acquitted. By 1872 she was the last resident at the Oyster Cove station and was promoted by colonists and scientists as the 'last' Aboriginal Tasmanian. After her death the Royal Society of Tasmania had her body exhumed (1878); her skeleton was displayed in the Tasmanian Museum until 1947 and was finally cremated and her ashes scattered in the D'Entrecasteaux Channel in 1976.",
 [('The "last" myth','Scholars and the Tasmanian Aboriginal community reject the idea that Aboriginal Tasmanians died out with Truganini. Fanny Cochrane Smith (died 1905), who recorded songs in a Tasmanian language on wax cylinders in 1899 and 1903, outlived her, and thousands of Aboriginal Tasmanians today descend from families who survived on the islands of Bass Strait and elsewhere.'),
  ('Collaborator or survivor?','Truganini has been portrayed both as having helped the colonial removals and as a resilient survivor making hard choices; historians debate her motives with limited sources in her own words.'),
  ('Bodies as specimens','Her treatment became a landmark in the campaign for the return of Aboriginal remains; in 2002 and 2005 the Royal College of Surgeons in England returned samples of her hair and skin.')],
 ['wiki:Truganini','wiki:Fanny_Cochrane_Smith','wiki:Black_War'],img='Truganini',end=1976,dateNote='c. 1812-1876; remains cremated 1976'),
X('arioi-and-oro','The Arioi: the travelling sacred society of the war god \'Oro',H,'confirmed',3,1650,['ethnohistoric accounts','oral tradition','archaeological'],
 "In the Society Islands (Tahiti, Ra'iatea and neighbours) of the 17th-18th centuries, the cult of the war god 'Oro, son of the creator Ta'aroa, became dominant, centred on the great marae (temple) of Taputapuatea on Ra'iatea, his legendary birthplace. Its most famous institution was the Arioi, an order of men and women from all social levels, said to have been founded by 'Oro, who had to be 'possessed' by the god to join. Arranged in ranks marked by tattoos, they travelled between islands in fleets of canoes, performing dances, dramas, songs and satire at festivals and religious ceremonies, and were honoured guests of chiefs. European visitors such as Bougainville and James Cook described them; Cook also witnessed a human sacrifice to 'Oro in 1777. The order declined after the conversion of the Tahitian ruler Pōmare II to Christianity in the 1810s.",
 [('Infanticide','European observers and missionaries reported that Arioi members were required to kill children born to them, which horrified the missionaries. Some scholars see this as a way to keep the order separate from family and lineage politics; others caution that outsiders\' accounts may have exaggerated or misunderstood rites they were barred from seeing.'),
  ('Actors, priests or spies?','The Arioi have been described variously as a religious order, a troupe of performing artists, a political network spreading the \'Oro cult, and an elite that enjoyed freedom from ordinary rules. Their esoteric teachings were secret, so their full role is unknown.'),
  ('Navigators and the god','The priest and navigator Tupaia, who sailed with Cook in 1769, came from Ra\'iatea\'s \'Oro priesthood; Taputapuatea, listed by UNESCO in 2017, was a gathering place for voyagers from across eastern Polynesia.')],
 ['wiki:Arioi','wiki:ʻOro','wiki:Taputapuatea_marae'],img='Arioi',end=1820,dateNote='17th century - c. 1820'),
X('taaroa-cosmic-egg','Ta\'aroa in the cosmic shell, and the statue of A\'a',M,'myth-legend',2,1821,['oral tradition','physical object','missionary records'],
 "In Tahitian tradition the creator Ta'aroa (known elsewhere in Polynesia as Tangaroa, Tagaloa or Kanaloa) existed alone in a shell like an egg, spinning in the endless void before there was sky, earth, sun or stars. Bored, he cracked it open, found only darkness and silence, and from the pieces of the shell made rocks, sand and the foundation of the world; his own body supplied the rest of creation. In Māori and other traditions Tangaroa is god of the sea and fish. A famous wooden figure from the island of Rurutu, covered with thirty small figures, has often been identified as Ta'aroa creating gods and people. It was handed to missionaries of the London Missionary Society on Ra'iatea in 1821 as a sign of the islanders' conversion to Christianity, and is now in the British Museum.",
 [('Ta\'aroa or an ancestor?','The missionary William Ellis identified the figure as Ta\'aroa, and that label became famous. Later researchers note there is little evidence for this: the islanders called it A\'a, a name not known from wider Polynesian cosmology, and anthropologist Steven Hooper argued it was made as a casket for the bones of a revered ancestor.'),
  ('A world egg','Creation from a cosmic egg or shell appears in many cultures, from the Chinese Pangu to the Indian Hiranyagarbha and the Finnish Kalevala; scholars debate whether such similarities reflect shared ancient ideas or independent invention.'),
  ('Art and empire','The figure was admired by 20th-century artists such as Picasso and Henry Moore, who owned casts; for Polynesians it is also a reminder of the destruction and removal of sacred objects during conversion.')],
 ['wiki:Taʼaroa','wiki:Tangaroa','wiki:Statue_of_A\'a_from_Rurutu'],img='File:Wooden figure of A\'a British Museum (2082156178).jpg',dateNote='Traditional; statue given to missionaries in 1821'),
]
# --- part 3: China and Central Asia
E+=[
X('zhou-conquest-mandate','The fall of the Shang and the Mandate of Heaven (c. 1046 BCE)',H,'confirmed',2,-1046,['textual','inscriptions','astronomical retrocalculation'],
 "Around 1046 BCE King Wu of Zhou, a western vassal of the Shang, defeated the last Shang king, Di Xin, at the Battle of Muye and took the Shang capital; Di Xin is said to have burned himself to death in his palace. The Zhou justified their conquest with a new idea: the Mandate of Heaven (Tianming). Heaven, they argued, grants the right to rule to a virtuous dynasty and withdraws it from a corrupt one, signalling its displeasure through disasters and omens. The idea, set out in early Zhou texts associated with the Duke of Zhou, became the central principle of Chinese political legitimacy for three thousand years and implied a right of rebellion against unjust rulers. A bronze vessel, the Li gui, excavated in 1976 in Shaanxi, records that King Wu attacked Shang on the morning of a jiazi day, a contemporary confirmation of the event.",
 [('The wicked last king','Later histories, including Sima Qian\'s, portray Di Xin as a monster who drank, tortured and was led astray by his consort Daji. Oracle-bone records from his reign show a ruler making normal ancestral sacrifices, and Daji does not appear in them; many historians see the stories as Zhou propaganda that grew with each retelling.'),
  ('Dating by the planets','Previous chronologies proposed at least 44 different dates, from 1130 to 1018 BCE. The government-sponsored Xia-Shang-Zhou Chronology Project (1996-2000) chose 1046 BCE, partly using a passage giving the positions of Jupiter and other bodies; some astronomers link the Zhou\'s claim to Heaven\'s favour to a striking gathering of the five visible planets in 1059 BCE, while others reject the project\'s methods and favour different dates.'),
  ('A sign in the sky?','The Li gui inscription contains the word sui, which some read as the planet Jupiter being in the right position for victory and others as the name of a sacrifice; the passage is still debated.')],
 ['wiki:Battle_of_Muye','wiki:Mandate_of_Heaven','wiki:Li_gui_(vessel)','wiki:Xia–Shang–Zhou_Chronology_Project'],img='File:Li Gui, 2016-09-09 01.jpg',dateNote='c. 1046 BCE (traditional dates range 1130-1018 BCE)'),
X('burning-of-books-213bce','The burning of the books and burying of the scholars (213-212 BCE)',H,'disputed',2,-213,['textual'],
 "According to Sima Qian's Records of the Grand Historian, written about a century later, after the First Emperor, Qin Shi Huang, unified China in 221 BCE his chancellor Li Si proposed in 213 BCE that all histories other than Qin's, together with the Classic of Poetry, the Book of Documents and the writings of the 'hundred schools' of philosophy, be burned, sparing only works on medicine, divination, agriculture and forestry and copies kept by official scholars. The next year, Sima Qian says, the emperor had more than 460 scholars buried alive after alchemists who had promised him an elixir of immortality deceived and criticised him. The story became the classic Chinese example of tyranny against learning, and later Confucian scholars blamed Qin for the loss of many ancient texts.",
 [('Exaggerated by the Han?','Modern historians agree that Qin confiscated and destroyed works it considered subversive, but many doubt the details. The account was written under the Han dynasty, which had reason to blacken its predecessor; some scholars argue that the victims were mainly fangshi (magicians and alchemists) rather than Confucians, and that the word translated "buried alive" may simply mean "killed".'),
  ('Who really destroyed the books?','Official copies survived the edict in the imperial library, but that library was burned when the rebel Xiang Yu sacked the Qin capital Xianyang in 206 BCE, which may have caused greater losses. Legend also says Confucian texts were hidden in walls, such as in the house of Confucius\'s descendants, and recovered under the Han, giving rise to long disputes between \'Old Text\' and \'New Text\' versions of the classics.'),
  ('A recurring pattern','The episode is often compared with other deliberate destructions of knowledge, from the burning of Maya books in 1562 to modern book burnings; critics warn against treating every such story as equally well documented.')],
 ['wiki:Burning_of_books_and_burying_of_scholars','wiki:Li_Si'],img='Burning of books and burying of scholars',end=-212,dateNote='213-212 BCE (according to Sima Qian, c. 94 BCE)'),
X('hua-mulan','Hua Mulan: the woman warrior of the Ballad',M,'myth-legend',2,450,['textual','literary history'],
 "The Ballad of Mulan is a short Chinese folk poem, probably composed in northern China during the Northern Wei period (386-535 CE), when the north was ruled by the Xianbei Tuoba clan and fought the nomadic Rouran. When the khagan calls up troops, Mulan disguises herself as a man to take her ageing father's place, serves for twelve years (the poem says her comrades never discovered she was a woman), refuses an official post and returns home to put on her old clothes. The poem was first collected in a 6th-century anthology, now lost, and survives in the Song-dynasty Yuefu Shiji. Later writers gave her the surname Hua and developed the story in plays, novels and, from 1998, Disney films.",
 [('Real person?','No reliable historical record mentions her, and she is absent from collections of exemplary women of the Northern Wei. Local traditions in several Chinese provinces claim her as a native and point to temples and graves, but historians generally regard her as a literary figure, perhaps inspired by real women soldiers.'),
  ('Han or Xianbei?','Because the poem calls the ruler "khagan" and is set in the Xianbei-ruled north, some scholars argue that Mulan was originally a Xianbei rather than Han Chinese heroine, and that her name may derive from a Xianbei word; others see the details as poetic convention.'),
  ('Many Mulans','Her image has shifted from a model of filial piety in imperial China, to a patriotic symbol in wartime, to a feminist icon; scholars use her changing story to trace changing ideals of gender and loyalty.')],
 ['wiki:Hua_Mulan'],img='Hua Mulan',dateNote='Ballad c. 4th-6th century CE; first collected 6th century'),
X('white-snake-leifeng','The Legend of the White Snake and the fall of Leifeng Pagoda (1924)',M,'myth-legend',2,1624,['textual','literary history','architectural'],
 "The Legend of the White Snake is one of China's four great folk legends. A white snake spirit who has cultivated human form for centuries, Bai Suzhen, falls in love with and marries a young man, Xu Xian, in Hangzhou, helped by her companion the green snake Xiaoqing. The Buddhist monk Fahai exposes her: at the Dragon Boat Festival she drinks realgar wine and reverts to snake form, frightening her husband to death, after which she steals a herb of immortality to revive him and floods Jinshan Temple to rescue him. Finally Fahai imprisons her under the Leifeng (Thunder Peak) Pagoda beside West Lake. The story grew from Song-era storytelling and was fixed in Feng Menglong's 1624 version. The real Leifeng Pagoda, built in 975, was weakened by people stealing its bricks, believed to cure illness or prevent miscarriage, and collapsed on 25 September 1924; it was rebuilt in 2002.",
 [('Demon or heroine?','Early versions present the snake as a dangerous seductress and Fahai as a protector; later operas and modern films make her a loyal wife and Fahai the villain, reflecting changing attitudes to love, gender and religious authority.'),
  ('A symbolic collapse','When the pagoda fell, the writer Lu Xun wrote essays welcoming its fall as the collapse of oppressive old thinking, linking it to the freeing of the imprisoned White Snake. Popular tradition held that she would be freed if the pagoda fell.'),
  ('Shared serpent-women','Folklorists compare her with other serpent-woman stories, such as the French Melusine and the Greek lamia, and debate whether they share origins or reflect a widespread motif.')],
 ['wiki:Legend_of_the_White_Snake','wiki:Leifeng_Pagoda'],img='Leifeng Pagoda',end=1924,dateNote='Story from Song era (12th-13th c.); Feng Menglong 1624; pagoda built 975, collapsed 1924'),
X('boxer-rebellion','The Boxer Uprising (1899-1901): fighters who believed bullets could not harm them',H,'confirmed',2,1899,['textual','photographic','official records'],
 "The Boxers, or 'Righteous and Harmonious Fists' (Yihequan), arose in Shandong in north China in the late 1890s amid drought, floods, poverty and resentment of foreign powers and Christian missionaries. They practised martial arts and rituals of spirit possession, and claimed that their charms and gods made them invulnerable to swords and bullets; they demonstrated this to recruits by firing guns loaded with blanks. Some blamed the drought on Christians, accusing them of offending local gods and disrupting feng shui. In June 1900 tens of thousands converged on Beijing, killing missionaries and many thousands of Chinese Christians, and the Empress Dowager Cixi declared war on the foreign powers. An Eight-Nation Alliance relieved the 55-day siege of Beijing's Legation Quarter on 14 August 1900. The Boxer Protocol of 1901 imposed an indemnity of 450 million taels of silver.",
 [('Magic that failed','The belief in invulnerability ended in massacres of Boxers by modern rifles and artillery. Historians see it as part of a long tradition of protective magic in Chinese sects, and compare it with similar beliefs in other anti-colonial movements, such as the Maji Maji rising in East Africa.'),
  ('Rumours','Rumours that missionaries kidnapped children and used their organs for medicine fuelled anti-Christian violence; such stories had circulated for decades and were not true.'),
  ('Many Boxers','Historian Paul Cohen showed how the uprising was remembered as myth in different ways: as superstitious fanatics in the West, as patriotic anti-imperialists in 20th-century Chinese nationalism and as feudal backwardness in others. The foreign armies\' own looting and reprisals are also part of the record.')],
 ['wiki:Boxer_Rebellion','wiki:Siege_of_the_International_Legations'],img='Boxer Rebellion',end=1901,dateNote='1899-1901; siege of the legations 20 June - 14 August 1900'),
X('red-cliffs-208','The Battle of Red Cliffs (208-209 CE): history versus the Romance of the Three Kingdoms',H,'confirmed',2,208,['textual'],
 "In the winter of 208-209 CE the warlord Cao Cao, who controlled the Han emperor and northern China, led a huge force south down the Yangtze to finish off his rivals. The allied southern warlords Sun Quan and Liu Bei, with Sun Quan's commander Zhou Yu, met him at Chibi ('Red Cliffs'). According to the historical Records of the Three Kingdoms, the general Huang Gai feigned surrender and sent ships filled with reeds, kindling and oil towards Cao Cao's fleet; set alight and driven by a south-east wind, they burned it, and Cao Cao's army, already suffering from disease, retreated north. The victory ensured that China would remain divided among the Three Kingdoms of Wei, Shu and Wu. The battle is best known through the 14th-century novel Romance of the Three Kingdoms, attributed to Luo Guanzhong.",
 [('Zhuge Liang\'s magic','In the novel, Liu Bei\'s strategist Zhuge Liang \'borrows\' 100,000 arrows by sending straw-covered boats into the fog for Cao Cao\'s archers to shoot at, and summons the east wind with rituals at an altar. These famous episodes are not in the historical sources and are generally regarded as fiction, or as credit taken from other people and events.'),
  ('How big an army?','Cao Cao claimed 800,000 men; Zhou Yu estimated around 230,000, and modern historians suggest far fewer. Disease, perhaps an epidemic such as schistosomiasis or typhus, may have hurt his army as much as fire.'),
  ('Where was it?','The location of the battlefield is still debated, with sites near Wuhan in Hubei and near Yueyang in Hunan among the candidates; a town in Hubei was officially renamed Chibi in 1998.')],
 ['wiki:Battle_of_Red_Cliffs','wiki:Romance_of_the_Three_Kingdoms'],img='Battle of Red Cliffs',end=209,dateNote='Winter 208-209 CE'),
X('fuxi-bagua','Fuxi, Nüwa and the eight trigrams that inspired Leibniz',M,'myth-legend',2,-2900,['textual','iconographic','oral tradition'],
 "Fuxi is a culture hero of Chinese myth, often paired with his sister and wife Nüwa. They are pictured with human upper bodies and intertwined snake tails, Fuxi holding a carpenter's square and Nüwa a compass, in tomb art from the Han dynasty and in silk paintings from Astana in Turpan (6th-8th centuries). Fuxi is credited with teaching people hunting, fishing, cooking and marriage, and with creating the bagua, the eight trigrams of broken and unbroken lines that underlie the I Ching (Book of Changes) divination system, after observing patterns in the heavens and earth. Traditional chronology placed him around 2900 BCE. In 1701 the Jesuit missionary Joachim Bouvet sent the philosopher Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz a diagram of the 64 hexagrams in the order arranged by the Song scholar Shao Yong. Leibniz, who had developed binary arithmetic, saw that the sequence matched binary numbers and in 1703 published his explanation of binary, citing the 'figures of Fuxi'.",
 [('Ancient binary?','Leibniz believed Fuxi had discovered binary arithmetic thousands of years earlier and that the knowledge had been lost. Historians of mathematics note that Shao Yong\'s 11th-century arrangement was not intended as a number system, and that assigning binary values to the hexagrams is a modern reading.'),
  ('After the flood','In flood myths recorded among the Miao and other peoples of southern China, Fuxi and Nüwa are a brother and sister who survive a great flood, often in a gourd, and marry to repopulate the world; some scholars think these stories fed into the classical myth, while others see separate traditions.'),
  ('Myth or memory of real ancestors?','Some Chinese historians have treated Fuxi as a legendary chief of a real prehistoric society; most scholars regard him as a mythical figure whose role was systematised by Han-dynasty writers.')],
 ['wiki:Fuxi','wiki:Bagua','wiki:I_Ching','wiki:Joachim_Bouvet'],img='File:Fuxi and Nüwa. 1967 Astana Cemetery.png',dateNote='Traditional date c. 2900 BCE; Leibniz\'s binary paper 1703'),
X('silk-secret-smuggled','The secret of silk: a goddess, a princess\'s headdress and two smuggling monks',H,'confirmed',2,552,['textual','archaeological','biomolecular'],
 "For millennia silk production was a Chinese speciality. Legend credits its discovery to Leizu, wife of the Yellow Emperor, when a cocoon fell into her tea and unravelled around 2700 BCE; archaeology shows silk was produced much earlier, and in 2016 chemists reported silk proteins in soil from 8,500-year-old tombs at Jiahu in Henan. Sericulture reached the oasis kingdom of Khotan by about the 1st century CE. The pilgrim Xuanzang recorded the local legend that a Chinese princess, sent to marry the king of Khotan, smuggled silkworm eggs and mulberry seeds out of China hidden in her headdress; a painted wooden panel found by Aurel Stein at Dandan Oilik seems to show the scene. In the West the historian Procopius tells how, around 552 CE, two monks who had lived in 'Serinda' brought silkworm eggs to Constantinople hidden in hollow canes at the request of Emperor Justinian I, founding the Byzantine silk industry.",
 [('Industrial espionage?','The monks\' story is often called the first recorded case of industrial espionage. Historians accept that silk production began in the Byzantine Empire around this time, but the details come from only a few sources, and the monks\' origin (probably Sogdiana or Central Asia rather than China itself) is debated.'),
  ('Princess legend','Some scholars see the Khotan princess story as a legend explaining how a technology spread through marriage alliances; whether a specific princess existed is unknown.'),
  ('How old is silk?','The Jiahu evidence is indirect, based on protein traces rather than surviving fabric, and could reflect wild silk; the oldest surviving silk fabrics are several thousand years younger.')],
 ['wiki:Smuggling_of_silkworm_eggs_into_the_East_Roman_Empire','wiki:Kingdom_of_Khotan','wiki:Leizu','wiki:Sericulture','doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0168042'],img='File:British Museum silk princess painting.jpg',dateNote='Legend c. 2700 BCE; Jiahu c. 6500 BCE; Khotan 1st century CE; Byzantium c. 552 CE'),
X('sima-qian-shiji','Sima Qian: the castrated historian who wrote China\'s first great history',H,'confirmed',2,-94,['textual','archaeological confirmation'],
 "Sima Qian (c. 145 - c. 86 BCE) was Grand Historian at the court of Emperor Wu of Han, an office that also involved astronomy and the calendar. In 99 BCE he alone defended the general Li Ling, who had surrendered to the Xiongnu after a failed campaign. The furious emperor sentenced him to death; unable to pay to commute the sentence, Sima Qian chose castration over suicide so that he could finish the history his father had begun. In his famous Letter to Ren An he called it the worst of all punishments. His Records of the Grand Historian (Shiji), completed around 94 BCE, covers some 2,500 years from the Yellow Emperor to his own time in 130 chapters, and set the model for all later Chinese official histories. In the early 20th century the scholar Wang Guowei showed that the list of Shang kings in the Shiji closely matched names on newly discovered oracle bones, confirming that it preserved genuine ancient records.",
 [('History or legend?','The Shiji mixes documented history with legends such as the Yellow Emperor and the Xia dynasty. The oracle-bone confirmation of the Shang kings led some scholars to argue that the Xia account may also rest on real tradition; others caution that there is no comparable written confirmation for the Xia.'),
  ('A biased witness?','Some historians argue that Sima Qian\'s portraits of Qin Shi Huang and Emperor Wu were shaped by Han politics and by his own suffering; others praise his use of sources, travel and interviews and his willingness to criticise the powerful.'),
  ('A silent rebuke','Readers have long seen hidden criticism of Emperor Wu in his accounts of earlier tyrants and in his biographies of assassins, rebels and ordinary people, which give unusual attention to those outside the court.')],
 ['wiki:Sima_Qian','wiki:Shiji','wiki:Wang_Guowei'],img='Sima Qian',end=-86,dateNote='c. 145 - c. 86 BCE; castrated 99 BCE; Shiji completed c. 94 BCE'),
X('merv-1221','Merv: once perhaps the world\'s largest city, destroyed by the Mongols (1221)',H,'confirmed',2,1221,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Merv, in today's Turkmenistan, was a great oasis city on the Silk Road for over two thousand years, with successive walled cities built beside one another, from the Achaemenid Persian period to the Seljuk Turks. Under the Seljuk sultan Ahmad Sanjar (ruled 1118-1157), whose great domed mausoleum still stands, it may have been the largest city in the world. In 1221, during Genghis Khan's invasion of the Khwarazmian Empire, Merv surrendered to his son Tolui. Contemporary historians wrote that the Mongols then massacred the population, sparing only a few hundred artisans: Ibn al-Athir reported some 700,000 dead and the Persian historian Juvayni over 1.3 million. Sanjar's tomb was burned and dug up in search of treasure. The city never fully recovered. Its ruins are a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
 [('How many died?','Modern historians regard the medieval death tolls as greatly exaggerated: they exceed most estimates of the city\'s population, which may itself have included refugees. Some researchers argue the numbers reflect the shock of the event and the rhetorical habits of chroniclers; others think a very large massacre did occur even if the figures are inflated.'),
  ('The end of a civilisation?','Some writers see the Mongol destruction of Merv, Nishapur, Balkh and Urgench as a catastrophe from which the Iranian and Central Asian world never fully recovered, also because of damage to irrigation; others point to recovery under later Mongol and Timurid rulers and to longer-term environmental and political causes of decline.'),
  ('Alexander\'s city','Merv was called Alexandria for a time in the Hellenistic era, but the story that Alexander the Great founded or visited it is regarded as legendary.')],
 ['wiki:Merv','wiki:Tomb_of_Ahmad_Sanjar','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/886/|UNESCO: State Historical and Cultural Park "Ancient Merv"|heritage listing'],img='Merv',dateNote='Seljuk peak 11th-12th century; Mongol sack 1221'),
]
# --- part 4: Near East, Norse, cryptids, hoaxes and Cold War secrets
E+=[
X('nabonidus-first-archaeologist','Nabonidus and Ennigaldi: the king who dug up the past and the first museum',H,'confirmed',2,-556,['textual','archaeological','cuneiform inscriptions'],
 "Nabonidus (ruled 556-539 BCE) was the last king of the Neo-Babylonian Empire. Devoted to the moon god Sin, whose temple at Harran his mother Adad-guppi served, he spent about ten years (c. 552-543 BCE) at the Arabian oasis of Tayma, leaving his son Belshazzar as regent in Babylon, until the city fell to Cyrus of Persia in 539 BCE. He excavated and restored ancient temples, searching for their original foundation deposits: at Sippar he reported finding the foundation inscription of the Akkadian king Naram-Sin, whom he dated to 3,200 years before his own time, and he had old inscriptions copied and restored. At Ur he installed his daughter Ennigaldi as high priestess of the moon god. In 1925 Leonard Woolley excavated rooms in her complex containing older objects, including a Kassite boundary stone and part of a statue of King Shulgi, with clay cylinders labelling them in several languages, which he interpreted as the world's first known museum (c. 530 BCE).",
 [('The first archaeologist?','Nabonidus is often called the first archaeologist. Historians note that his digging was religious and political, aimed at legitimising his rule by connecting it with ancient kings; his date for Naram-Sin was too early by well over a thousand years.'),
  ('A museum or a storeroom?','Woolley\'s \'museum\' interpretation is popular but debated: the objects with \'labels\' may have been a temple collection of antiquities, a school collection or simply stored relics.'),
  ('The mad king of Daniel','Babylonian texts hostile to Nabonidus, written after the Persian conquest, accuse him of neglecting the god Marduk. A Dead Sea Scrolls text, the Prayer of Nabonidus, describes him being afflicted for seven years at Tayma; many scholars think the Book of Daniel\'s story of Nebuchadnezzar\'s madness was originally about Nabonidus.')],
 ['wiki:Nabonidus','wiki:Ennigaldi-Nanna\'s_museum','wiki:Ennigaldi-Nanna'],img='Nabonidus',end=-530,dateNote='Reigned 556-539 BCE; museum c. 530 BCE; found 1925'),
X('behemoth-and-leviathan','Behemoth and Leviathan: the beasts of Job - hippo, crocodile, chaos monster or dinosaur?',M,'myth-legend',2,-500,['textual','comparative mythology'],
 "In the Book of Job (chapters 40-41), God answers Job from a whirlwind by describing two awesome creatures. Behemoth eats grass like an ox, has bones like bronze and 'moves his tail like a cedar'; Leviathan is a fire-breathing, armoured sea monster that no human can tame. Leviathan also appears in the Psalms and in Isaiah, where God will slay 'Leviathan the twisting serpent' at the end of days. Leviathan echoes the seven-headed sea serpent Lotan defeated by Baal in Ugaritic myth (c. 1300 BCE), and both creatures recall ancient Near Eastern stories of a god defeating chaos monsters, such as Marduk and Tiamat. In later Jewish tradition, starting with the Book of Enoch, Behemoth is the male land monster and Leviathan the female sea monster, and their flesh will be served at a banquet for the righteous at the end of time.",
 [('Real animals','Many commentators since at least the Middle Ages, and much modern scholarship, identify Behemoth with the hippopotamus (perhaps from an Egyptian term for \'water ox\') or the elephant, and Leviathan with the Nile crocodile or the whale, seeing the poems as poetic exaggerations of real beasts.'),
  ('Mythic chaos beasts','Other scholars argue they are primarily mythological symbols of untamed chaos that only God can control, drawing on Canaanite and Mesopotamian traditions.'),
  ('Dinosaurs?','Some young-earth creationists, such as the organisation Answers in Genesis, argue that Behemoth was a sauropod dinosaur, mainly because of the tail \'like a cedar\', and that Leviathan may have been a marine reptile, implying that humans and dinosaurs lived together. Mainstream biblical scholars and palaeontologists reject this, noting that non-avian dinosaurs died out about 66 million years before humans appeared.')],
 ['wiki:Behemoth','wiki:Leviathan','wiki:Lotan'],img='File:William Blake - Behemoth and Leviathan - Google Art Project.jpg',dateNote='Book of Job c. 6th-4th century BCE; Ugaritic Lotan c. 1300 BCE'),
X('oseberg-ship','The Oseberg ship burial: two women and the richest Viking grave',H,'confirmed',2,834,['archaeological','dendrochronology','skeletal analysis'],
 "In 1904-05 archaeologists Gabriel Gustafson and Haakon Shetelig excavated a great burial mound at Oseberg near Tønsberg in Norway. Sealed in blue clay lay a beautifully carved oak ship about 21.5 m long, which tree-ring dating shows was buried in the autumn of 834 CE. In a burial chamber on deck were the skeletons of two women, one about 80 years old with severe arthritis and the other about 50, along with an ornately carved wagon (the only complete Viking Age wagon known), four sledges, beds, chests, textiles and tapestries, kitchen equipment, and the remains of horses, dogs and oxen. A wooden bucket has a handle mount of two cross-legged enamelled figures, nicknamed the 'Buddha bucket'. The ship and finds are displayed in Oslo.",
 [('Queen Åsa?','The high-ranking woman has often been identified with Queen Åsa, grandmother of Harald Fairhair according to the sagas; this cannot be proven, and some scholars doubt that saga genealogies preserve reliable details from the 9th century.'),
  ('Mistress and servant, or two equals?','The traditional interpretation is that one woman was sacrificed to accompany the other, as described in Ibn Fadlan\'s account of a Rus\' funeral. Others note both women were buried with care in one bed, had diets rich in meat, and may both have been of high status, perhaps a priestess.'),
  ('Distant roots and doubtful DNA','A study reported that the younger woman had a mitochondrial lineage common in Iran and the Black Sea region, but later attempts failed to confirm it, and the samples may be contaminated. The grave was also robbed in antiquity, so some valuables are missing.')],
 ['wiki:Oseberg_ship'],img='Oseberg ship',dateNote='Buried autumn 834 CE (tree-ring dating); excavated 1904-05'),
X('uppsala-temple','The Temple at Uppsala: golden chains, sacred groves and nine-year sacrifices',H,'disputed',2,1070,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Around 1075 the German cleric Adam of Bremen described a great pagan temple at Uppsala (today Gamla Uppsala) in Sweden, adorned with gold and surrounded by a golden chain. Inside stood statues of three gods: Thor, the mightiest, in the centre, with Wodan (Odin) and Fricco (Freyr) beside him. Every nine years, he wrote, a festival lasting nine days was held at the spring equinox, at which nine males of every kind of creature, including humans, were sacrificed and hung in the sacred grove beside the temple. Near the temple stood a huge evergreen tree and a spring into which a living man was thrown. Gamla Uppsala was certainly a major royal and religious centre: it has huge royal burial mounds of the 6th century, and excavations have revealed large halls and long lines of timber posts.",
 [('Eyewitness or propaganda?','Adam never visited Uppsala and relied on informants; historians note that his description echoes the biblical Temple of Solomon, and Henrik Janson argued that it served church politics of the time, portraying the Swedes as heathens in need of Hamburg-Bremen\'s authority. Others think it preserves real memories of pre-Christian cult practices.'),
  ('Where was the temple?','In 1926 the archaeologist Sune Lindqvist found postholes under the medieval church that were long reconstructed as the temple. More recent research suggests the reconstructions overestimate the building, and that the cult may have centred on a large hall used for feasting rather than a separate temple.'),
  ('Human sacrifice','Archaeological evidence for human sacrifice at Uppsala is limited, though animal sacrifices are well attested at Scandinavian cult sites; sources such as Thietmar of Merseburg describe similar nine-yearly sacrifices at Lejre in Denmark.')],
 ['wiki:Temple_at_Uppsala','wiki:Gamla_Uppsala','wiki:Adam_of_Bremen'],img='Temple at Uppsala',dateNote='Described c. 1075; site in use from c. 5th century'),
X('minnesota-iceman','The Minnesota Iceman: a "missing link" frozen in a carnival freezer',H,'debunked',3,1968,['eyewitness','photographic','investigative reports'],
 "From 1967 the showman Frank Hansen toured fairs and shopping malls in the US and Canada with a refrigerated coffin containing a hairy, man-like figure frozen in a block of ice, billed as a possible 'missing link'. In December 1968 the zoologists and cryptid researchers Ivan Sanderson and Bernard Heuvelmans examined it for several days through the ice. They concluded that it was a real body and Heuvelmans described it in a scientific bulletin as a new species of hominid, which he called Homo pongoides. Sanderson publicised it in Argosy magazine (1969). The Smithsonian Institution became interested, but Hansen withdrew the exhibit, saying it belonged to an anonymous Californian millionaire, and later displayed a 'replacement' model. The primatologist John Napier, working with the Smithsonian, reported that Hansen had commissioned a latex figure from a West Coast company in 1967 and concluded that the 'original' and the 'replacement' were the same model. In 2013 the exhibit was sold on eBay and later displayed in a museum of oddities in Austin, Texas.",
 [('A real hominid?','Heuvelmans maintained until his death that he and Sanderson had seen a real corpse, citing details such as the smell of decay and wounds, and suggested a switch had taken place. Some cryptozoologists still consider the case unresolved.'),
  ('Shifting stories','Hansen changed his account of the specimen\'s origin several times, including that it had been found in Siberian ice and that he had shot it himself; sceptics see this as part of the showman\'s promotion.'),
  ('Why experts were fooled','The case is often cited as a lesson in how viewing an object through cloudy ice, without being allowed to touch or sample it, can mislead even experienced zoologists.')],
 ['wiki:Minnesota_Iceman','wiki:Bernard_Heuvelmans','wiki:Ivan_T._Sanderson'],img='File:Minnesota-Iceman-composite-in-ice-and-reconstruction-600-px-tiny-Dec-2016-Darren-Naish-Tetrapod-Zoology.jpg',dateNote='Exhibited 1967-1970s; examined December 1968; sold 2013'),
X('mkultra','MKUltra: the CIA\'s secret mind-control experiments',H,'confirmed',2,1953,['government documents','congressional testimony'],
 "Project MKUltra was a programme of the US Central Intelligence Agency, authorised by Director Allen Dulles in April 1953 and led by the chemist Sidney Gottlieb, to research drugs and techniques that might be used for interrogation, mind control and weakening or discrediting people. During the Cold War, fears that Communist countries had developed 'brainwashing' drove the research. Under the cover of other organisations, the CIA funded more than 150 subprojects at over 80 universities, hospitals, prisons and companies, testing LSD and other drugs, hypnosis, sensory deprivation and electroshock, often on people who did not know they were being tested. In 1973 CIA Director Richard Helms ordered the MKUltra files destroyed. The programme was revealed by the Rockefeller Commission and the Church Committee in 1975, and in 1977 a Freedom of Information request found some 20,000 surviving financial documents, leading to Senate hearings.",
 [('Deaths and victims','The army scientist Frank Olson died in 1953 after falling from a New York hotel window days after he was secretly given LSD; the government later paid his family compensation, but his family argued he was murdered, which the CIA denies. In Montreal, psychiatrist Donald Ewen Cameron\'s \'psychic driving\' experiments, partly CIA-funded, left patients with lasting harm, and Canada and the US later paid some victims.'),
  ('Did it work?','Investigators found no evidence that MKUltra achieved reliable mind control, and the CIA said the research largely failed. Because most records were destroyed, critics argue the full scope cannot be known.'),
  ('Conspiracy magnet','Because MKUltra was a real conspiracy that was denied for decades, it is frequently cited in other theories, for example about assassins, cults and famous criminals; most of these alleged connections are unproven.')],
 ['wiki:MKUltra','wiki:Church_Committee','wiki:Frank_Olson'],img='MKUltra',end=1973,dateNote='1953-1973 (files destroyed 1973); revealed 1975-77'),
X('stargate-remote-viewing','The Stargate Project: the US government\'s psychic spies (1972-1995)',H,'confirmed',2,1978,['government documents','experiments'],
 "Worried by reports that the Soviet Union was funding 'psychotronic' research, US intelligence agencies paid for experiments in remote viewing, the claimed ability to describe distant places or hidden targets using the mind alone. Research began in 1972 at the Stanford Research Institute (SRI) in California, where the physicists Russell Targ and Harold Puthoff tested subjects including the artist Ingo Swann and, briefly, Uri Geller. From the late 1970s an army unit at Fort Meade, Maryland, used 'viewers' to try to locate hostages, weapons sites and missing aircraft, under code names such as Gondola Wish, Grill Flame, Center Lane, Sun Streak and finally Star Gate. In 1995 a review commissioned by the CIA from the American Institutes for Research concluded that remote viewing had never provided actionable intelligence, and the programme was ended and declassified.",
 [('Statistically real?','The statistician Jessica Utts, one of the 1995 reviewers, concluded that laboratory results showed statistically significant effects that deserved further research. The psychologist Ray Hyman, the other reviewer, argued that the apparent effects could be explained by methodological flaws and had never been independently replicated.'),
  ('Hits and misses','Former viewers such as Joseph McMoneagle cite apparent successes; critics point out that vague descriptions can be interpreted to fit after the fact, and that the unit\'s operational record showed no reliable results.'),
  ('A Cold War mirror','Historians note that the programme was driven as much by fear of a \'psychic gap\' with the Soviet Union as by evidence. The story inspired the book and film The Men Who Stare at Goats.')],
 ['wiki:Stargate_Project_(U.S._Army_unit)','wiki:Remote_viewing','wiki:Russell_Targ'],img='File:Russell Targ, physicist.jpg',end=1995,dateNote='SRI research from 1972; army unit 1978-1995; declassified 1995'),
X('operation-paperclip','Operation Paperclip: Nazi scientists who built America\'s rockets',H,'confirmed',2,1945,['government documents','textual'],
 "After the Second World War the United States secretly brought more than 1,600 German scientists, engineers and technicians to work for the US government between 1945 and 1959, in a programme known as Operation Paperclip. Many had worked on Nazi rocket, aviation, and chemical and biological weapons programmes. The best known was Wernher von Braun, who had led development of the V-2 rocket and later directed NASA's Marshall Space Flight Center, where his team built the Saturn V rocket that carried Apollo astronauts to the Moon. President Truman approved the programme in 1946 on condition that no active Nazis would be admitted, but officials rewrote or overlooked the records of many recruits. The Soviet Union ran its own programme: in Operation Osoaviakhim, on 22 October 1946, Soviet units rounded up German specialists and their families and moved them to the USSR.",
 [('Science versus justice','The V-2 was produced at the underground Mittelwerk factory by prisoners of the Mittelbau-Dora concentration camp, thousands of whom died. Arthur Rudolph, who managed V-2 production and later the Saturn V programme, left the US in 1984 after investigators confronted him over his role; critics say the programme let people complicit in war crimes escape justice.'),
  ('A necessary choice?','Defenders argue that the knowledge gained shaped US missiles and the space programme and kept it out of Soviet hands, and that von Braun and others were scientists caught up in the Nazi system rather than ideologues; historians continue to debate how much von Braun knew of and was responsible for the abuses.'),
  ('Paperclip in conspiracy lore','Because it was secret and real, Paperclip features in many conspiracy theories, for example about UFOs and secret aircraft; documented history does not support these claims.')],
 ['wiki:Operation_Paperclip','wiki:Arthur_Rudolph','wiki:Mittelwerk','wiki:Wernher_von_Braun'],img='Operation Paperclip',end=1959,dateNote='1945-1959'),
X('tiara-of-saitaphernes','The Tiara of Saitaphernes: the Louvre\'s golden fake (1896)',H,'debunked',3,1896,['physical object','museum records','confession'],
 "On 1 April 1896 the Louvre announced that it had bought, for 200,000 gold francs, a magnificent gold tiara said to have been given by the Greek city of Olbia on the Black Sea to the Scythian king Saitaphernes around the 3rd century BCE; a Greek inscription honoured 'the great and invincible King Saitaphernes'. Some scholars, including the German archaeologist Adolf Furtwängler, doubted it at once, pointing out stylistic mistakes and the lack of signs of age. In fact two dealers, the Hochmann brothers, had commissioned it in 1894 from Israel Rouchomovsky, a skilled goldsmith in Odesa, telling him it was a gift for an archaeologist friend. When the scandal reached him, Rouchomovsky travelled to Paris in 1903 and, after museum experts refused to believe him, proved his authorship by reproducing part of the tiara.",
 [('Victim or accomplice?','Rouchomovsky said he had not known his work would be sold as ancient, and after the scandal he became famous and received commissions in Paris. The dealers who sold it were never successfully prosecuted.'),
  ('Experts fooled','The case became a classic warning about how desire for spectacular finds can override scepticism; it led museums to adopt more careful authentication. The tiara is now displayed as a famous fake, and in 1997 the Israel Museum borrowed it for an exhibition on Rouchomovsky.'),
  ('A pattern','It is often compared with other celebrated forgeries such as the Piltdown Man and the Glozel finds, in which skilled fakes exploited the expectations of experts.')],
 ['wiki:Tiara_of_Saitaferne'],img='Tiara of Saitaferne',end=1903,dateNote='Made 1894; bought by the Louvre 1896; exposed 1903'),
X('palomares-1966','Palomares 1966: the lost hydrogen bombs of Spain',H,'confirmed',2,1966,['official records','environmental monitoring','eyewitness'],
 "On 17 January 1966 a US Air Force B-52G bomber on a Cold War airborne alert mission collided with a KC-135 tanker during mid-air refuelling over the Mediterranean coast near the Spanish village of Palomares in Almería. All four tanker crew and three of the bomber's seven crew died. The B-52 was carrying four B28 thermonuclear bombs. Three fell on land: their parachutes failed on two, whose conventional explosives detonated on impact without a nuclear explosion, scattering plutonium over about 2 square kilometres of farmland. The fourth fell into the sea. A local fisherman, Francisco Simó Orts, had seen it descend, and after an 80-day search involving the US Navy and the submersible Alvin it was located by Alvin on 17 March, lost again during lifting, and finally recovered intact on 7 April. To reassure the public and tourism, the Spanish information minister Manuel Fraga and the US ambassador Angier Biddle Duke swam in the sea there on 8 March 1966. About 1,750 tonnes of contaminated soil were shipped to the US.",
 [('How safe?','Spanish and US authorities have monitored residents for decades and report no clear health effects; residual plutonium contamination remained in fenced areas, and in 2015 the two countries signed an agreement to remove more soil, but as of 2025 the clean-up had not been fully completed.'),
  ('The clean-up crews','About 1,600 US airmen, many with little protection, cleaned up the site. A 2016 New York Times investigation found high rates of cancer among veterans it could trace; the Air Force had long maintained that exposures were safe, and whether the illnesses were caused by plutonium is hard to prove. In 2022 the US PACT Act gave Palomares veterans a presumption of radiation exposure for benefits.'),
  ('Broken Arrows','Palomares is one of the best documented \'Broken Arrow\' nuclear weapons accidents; others, such as Goldsboro in 1961 and Thule in Greenland in 1968, revealed how close accidents came to disaster, and how much was kept secret.')],
 ['wiki:1966_Palomares_accident','wiki:Manuel_Fraga','url:https://www.nytimes.com/2016/06/20/us/decades-later-sickness-among-airmen-after-a-hydrogen-bomb-accident.html|New York Times (2016): Decades Later, Sickness Among Airmen After a Hydrogen Bomb Accident|news','url:https://law.yale.edu/yls-today/news/56-years-later-palomares-veterans-win-va-recognition-radiation-exposure|Yale Law School (2022): 56 Years Later, Palomares Veterans Win VA Recognition of Radiation Exposure|news'],img='1966 Palomares accident',dateNote='17 January 1966; bomb recovered from the sea 7 April 1966'),
]
